<?php

declare(strict_types=1);

/**
 * Descriptions of the images, charts and diagrams in each framework PDF,
 * keyed by document slug then 1-indexed page number (matching the PDF's own
 * pagination, not any printed page number if the two differ).
 *
 * Used by the "View as Text" screen-reader alternative in pages/document.php.
 * pdf.js only extracts the real text layer - it has no idea a page contains
 * a bar chart or a photograph, so image-only pages fall back to a generic
 * "may be an image" notice and mixed pages silently drop the visual
 * altogether. An entry here fills that gap: for an image-only page it
 * replaces the generic fallback, for a page that also has real paragraph
 * text it is appended after it.
 *
 * Written by hand (or by an agent that actually looked at each page) - never
 * guess a description for a page that hasn't been reviewed, since a wrong
 * description is worse than the honest "may be an image" fallback it would
 * replace.
 */
return [
    'blueprint' => [
        1 => "Illustrated cover graphic in outline over a silhouette map of Mauritius: a smartphone platform with people chatting on a sofa, a giant open book with tiny figures arranging webpage elements, a person facing a smartphone login screen surrounded by security icons (shield, key, envelope, credit card), and three colored skyscraper bars beside a stylized building with a circuit-board tree, symbolizing digital transformation themes of connectivity, e-services, security and smart infrastructure.",
        2 => "A formal portrait photograph of a man in a grey suit and red tie, standing in front of the Mauritius national flag, alongside the national coat of arms, next to the heading \"The President of the Republic of Mauritius\".",
        4 => "A formal portrait photograph of a man wearing glasses, a dark suit, and a red tie, standing in front of the Mauritius national flag, alongside the national coat of arms, next to the heading \"Prime Minister of the Republic of Mauritius\".",
        6 => "A formal portrait photograph of a man in a navy suit and pink tie, standing indoors, next to the national coat of arms and the heading \"Minister of Information Technology, Communication and Innovation\".",
        8 => "A formal portrait photograph of a woman with curly hair wearing a white embroidered outfit, standing in front of a blue UN emblem backdrop, next to the heading \"Resident Representative for Mauritius and Seychelles\".",
        10 => "A table-of-contents infographic showing 14 colored diamond page-number markers connected by a dotted snaking line across three rows. In order: 11 Executive Summary, 18 Vision and strategic objectives, 20 Digital readiness and context, 21 Pillar 1: The foundation - State-of-the-art Info-structure, 26 Pillar 2: Human capital - Digital skills for all, 29 Pillar 3: Economy - driving innovation and private sector growth, 33 Pillar 4: Planet - building a sustainable and resilient digital future, 36 Enabler 1: Digital public infrastructure, 39 Enabler 2: Legal and regulatory reform, 42 Enabler 3: Institutional coordination and governance, 45 Enabler 4: Cybersecurity and trust, 49 Enabler 5: Data Governance and AI, 53 Governance, monitoring and implementation framework, 54 Final thoughts - Empowering the Future.",
        11 => "Section-divider illustration for the Executive Summary: a computer monitor showing a person holding a megaphone next to a stylized webpage layout, with decorative green splatter and star shapes beside it.",
        12 => "Figure 1: a circular flowchart titled \"Blueprint Elaboration Process\" with six labelled steps arranged around a central hub: Anchoring (grounded the Blueprint on OECD principles, national strategies and global best practices), Diagnostic (analysed current state of digitalisation, critical gaps and missing foundational elements), Readiness Assessment (assessed digital infrastructure, services, skills and maturity levels), Consultation (engaged private sector, ministries, departments, agencies, citizens, industry associations and academia), eDiplomacy (guidance from UN agencies on approach and formulation), and Strategic Framing (translated insights into strategic pillars, enablers and priority action areas). Flanking boxes list inputs on the left (National Policy Documents, OECD Principles of Digital Government, International Best Practices) and outputs on the right (Pillars and Enablers, Recommendations, Roadmap, Indicators). A banner beneath reads \"Overarching Methodology\", followed by three summary text boxes describing the rationale, inclusiveness and outcome of the process.",
        13 => "An infographic of four classical column icons, each representing one strategic pillar. Pillar 1 (grey), The Foundation: State-of-the-art Info-structure - delivering simple, fast and secure G2C, G2G and G2B services through a unified portal and super app built around life events, powered by Mobile ID, digital documents and proactive communication. Pillar 2 (pink), Human Capital - Digital Skills for All - empowering all citizens with tools, access and knowledge to go digital through inclusion, national literacy campaigns, targeted upskilling and universal access. Pillar 3 (magenta), Economy - Innovation and Private Sector Growth - helping businesses go digital, scaling startup ecosystems and enabling public-private-people innovation through data-driven policy and data exchange leveraging Freedom of Information. Pillar 4 (green), Planet - A Sustainable and Resilient Digital Future - greening digital infrastructure, reducing and recycling digital waste, promoting eco-practices and using technology for climate action and smart living through the Internet of Things.",
        14 => "An infographic titled \"National Enablers\" showing five colored gear icons with labels: Digital Public Infrastructure (blue) - a unified government stack for identity, data, payments and digital services; Legal and Regulatory Reform (pink) - updated legal frameworks to reinforce privacy and digital rights; Institutional Governance (green) - a streamlined coordination model with stronger delivery capacity and data governance; Cyber Resilience and Trust (yellow) - a national digital safety mechanism protecting people, systems, institutions and digital sovereignty; and Data Governance and AI (light blue) - a national data governance framework to empower citizens and embed data-driven decision making. Below is a banner reading \"Government Programme 2025-2029\" with a target icon.",
        15 => "A diagram titled \"The Architecture for Change\" depicting a temple: a triangular pediment reading \"Digitally empowered and globally competitive Mauritius\" rests atop four numbered columns labelled Pillar 1 The Foundation: State-of-the-Art Info-Structure, Pillar 2 Human Capital: Digital Skills for All, Pillar 3 Economy: Innovation and Private Sector Growth, and Pillar 4 Planet: A Sustainable and Resilient Digital Future. The columns stand on a stacked foundation of five horizontal bars labelled Digital Public Infrastructure, Legal and Regulatory Reform, Institutional Governance, Cyber Resilience and Trust, and Data Governance and AI. A side note states that monitoring and evaluation mechanisms are embedded across all stages.",
        16 => "A table titled \"Sustainable Development Goals\" listing SDGs 1 through 11, each with its official colored UN SDG icon, the relevant Blueprint pillars/enablers, and a remark explaining the link, e.g. SDG 1 No Poverty linked to Pillars 2 and 3, SDG 4 Quality Education linked to Pillars 1 and 2, SDG 9 Industry Innovation and Infrastructure linked to Pillars 1, 3 and 4, and so on.",
        17 => "Continuation of the Sustainable Development Goals table (SDGs 12-17) with colored UN SDG icons, mapped pillars/enablers and remarks. Below it, a circular wheel diagram illustrates the OECD Six Dimensions of Digital Government around a central icon of a bank/government building: 1 Digital by Design, 2 Data-Driven, 3 Government as a Platform, 4 Open by Default, 5 User-Driven, and 6 Proactiveness, arranged clockwise as colored wedge segments.",
        18 => "Section-divider illustration for Vision and Strategic Objectives: an open book from which colored bar-chart-like towers and a webpage panel rise, with two tiny figures interacting with the webpage and a person sitting reading a laptop nearby, surrounded by outline icons of an eye, a staircase, a gem and a target.",
        37 => "A layered architecture diagram titled \"Building Blocks of Government Stack\", shown as a stack of horizontal layers flanked by vertical bars labelled Artificial Intelligence (left) and Security and Resilience (right). From bottom to top the layers are: Infrastructure, Identity and Trust, Secure Information Sharing, Digital Service, Omni-channel digital services (split into G2C, G2B and G2G blocks), and Whole of Govt, Life, Enterprise at the top. Callouts on the left list inputs such as Health, Education, Social Security, Passport and Immigration, Judiciary, Online social engagement, and Smart/Mobile ID; callouts on the right list outputs such as unified Govt App/Chatbot on mobile and desktop devices, InfoHighway Data Sharing, Citizen Data Hub, Government Email/SMS Gateway, Citizen document Cloud, Government Intranet Systems (GINS) and Government Online Centre (GOC).",
        44 => "An organizational flowchart centered on \"Citizen Hub (DMO)\" showing relationships between institutional entities with directional arrows. Business and METC feed technological support and data to the AI Office and CSO. DTB implements and DTSO supports the Citizen Hub, which connects to Digital services (secured and protected by NCRCSA). On the right, NCRCSA governs authentication/access, MDPA promotes services hosted on Govt Cloud, DPO oversees data protection, and CERT.MU and ITSU provide security implementation, advice and audit, all connected in a network of labelled arrows showing data, authentication and support flows between the boxes.",
        51 => "A hub-and-spoke wheel diagram centered on \"Citizen Data Hub (CDH)\", connected by bidirectional arrows to eight surrounding sectors: Health, Education, Social Security, MNIC/Civil Status, Passport and Immigration, Justice, Transport, and Housing, each with its own icon, indicating two-way data flow between the central hub and each government domain. Beside it, a decorative illustration shows server racks feeding data into a laptop displaying line, bar and pie charts.",
        53 => "A two-column table titled the Digital Transformation Scorecard, listing six themes with sample indicators: 1. Citizen-Centric Services - percent of services online, satisfaction scores; 2. Digital Inclusion and Literacy - percent of population trained, women/PwD reach; 3. Public Sector Transformation - percent digital-by-design services, legacy systems retired; 4. Data-Driven Government - number of data-sharing use cases, percent analytics adoption; 5. Digital Economy Growth - number of startups supported, ICT share of GDP; 6. Cybersecurity and Trust - audit coverage, threat response time, awareness.",
        56 => "Back cover illustration: a white silhouette of the island of Mauritius rendered as a printed-circuit-board pattern with connected nodes and traces, above the title \"Digital Transformation - A Blueprint for Mauritius - A Bridge to the Future\" and the credit \"Ministry of Information Technology, Communication and Innovation\".",
